.TH SWAMP_BOT 1 "September 2026" "swamp_bot" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
swamp_bot \- read stdin and reply with one snarky chatbot response
.SH SYNOPSIS
\&./swampscheme scripts/swamp_bot.scm
.br
\&./swampscheme scripts/bad_bod.scm
.br
\&./swampscheme scripts/swamp_bot.scm < message.txt
.br
\&./swampscheme scripts/swamp_bot.scm <<< "hello swamp bot"
.br
\&./swamp_bot_chat.sh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B swamp_bot
reads all text from standard input, checks it against simple rules, and prints
exactly one response.
.PP
It is intentionally small: edit the Scheme file, add rules, run it again.
.PP
.B scripts/swamp_bot_lib.scm
contains the response rules.
.B scripts/swamp_bot.scm
and
.B scripts/bad_bod.scm
are one-shot entrypoints.
.B scripts/swamp_bot_chat.scm
is a Scheme chat loop, launched by
.B swamp_bot_chat.sh.
When the one-shot bot sees an exit request, it prints the exit response and
returns status
.B 86.
.SH TOPICS
.B swamp_bot
now has response buckets for greetings, identity checks, thanks, compliments,
insults, C, suckless software, Scheme/Lisp resentment, editors, Microsoft and
Windows 11, literal windows, Linux, FreeBSD, OpenBSD, Stardew Valley,
Daggerfall, fantasy opinions, math, coding, food, weather, time, commands,
exit requests, questions, blank input, and general fallback nonsense.
.SH EXAMPLES
Send a string directly:
.PP
.nf
\&./swampscheme scripts/swamp_bot.scm <<< "hello swamp bot"
.fi
.PP
Pipe text into it:
.PP
.nf
printf '%s\\n' "what are you?" | ./swampscheme scripts/swamp_bot.scm
.fi
.PP
Read from a file:
.PP
.nf
\&./swampscheme scripts/swamp_bot.scm < message.txt
.fi
.PP
Run it like a tiny chatbot:
.PP
.nf
\&./swamp_bot_chat.sh
.fi
.SH EXTENDING
Open
.B scripts/swamp_bot_lib.scm
and edit
.B swamp-bot-response.
Rules are checked from top to bottom in a
.B cond
form.
.PP
Put top-level overrides first when a topic must beat every other response
bucket:
.PP
.nf
((contains-token-any? text '("aardvark" "aardvarks"))
 "aardvarks are anteaters with better branding.")
.fi
.PP
Add new keyword responses before the final
.B else
rule:
.PP
.nf
((string-contains text "weather")
 "look outside. swamp bot is not a cloud.")

((string-contains text "name")
 "my name is swamp bot. unfortunately.")

((string-contains text "joke")
 "no.")
.fi
.PP
The final
.B else
is the fallback response used when no earlier rule matches.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal one-shot response.
.TP
.B 86
The input asked swamp bot to exit, quit, die, shut down, terminate, or halt.
.SH RANDOM RESPONSES
To make a response list:
.PP
.nf
(define bad-responses
  '("interesting. badly done."
    "swamp bot has considered this and rejected it."
    "that input had consequences."))
.fi
.PP
Then use Swamp's built-in random helpers:
.PP
.nf
(define (random-choice items)
  (list-ref items (random-integer (length items))))
.fi
.PP
Use it in the fallback:
.PP
.nf
(else
 (random-choice bad-responses))
.fi
.SH NOTES
The shell form
.PP
.nf
\&./swampscheme scripts/swamp_bot.scm < "hello swamp bot"
.fi
.PP
means "read from a file named hello swamp bot".
Use
.B <<<
to pass a literal string.
